{{smaller block|of ''tranquillizing'' his heart. {{right|''R. Hall.''}}}}

=Syn. {{smaller|To quiet, still, soothe, calm, lull, hush.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To become tranquil; also, to exert a quieting or calming effect.

{{smaller block|I'll try as I ride in my chariot to ''tranquilise.''
{{right|''Richardson'', Clarissa Harlowe, III. lviii.}}}}

Also spelled ''tranquilise, tranquillise.''

'''tranquilizer, tranquillizer''' (trang′kwil-ī-zėr), ''n.'' [< ''tranquilize'' + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] One who or that which tranquilizes. Also spelled ''tranquiliser, tranquilliser.''

'''tranquilizingly, tranquillizingly''' (trang′-kwil-ī-zing-li), ''adv.'' So as to tranquilize.

'''tranquillamente''' (träng-kēl-lä-men′te), ''adv.'' [It., < ''tranquillo'', tranquil: see ''tranquil.''] In ''music'', tranquilly; calmly; in a quiet manner.

'''tranquillity''' (trang-kwil′i̤-ti), ''n.'' [< ME. ''tranquillitee'', < OF. ''tranquillite'', F. ''tranquillité'' = Pr. ''tranquillitat, tranquilitat'' = Sp. ''tranquilidad'' = Pg. ''tranquillidade'' = It. ''tranquillità'', < L. ''tranquillita(t-)s'', tranquilness, < ''tranquillus'', tranquil: see ''tranquil.''] The state or character of being tranquil; quietness; serenity; freedom from disturbance or agitation; calmness.

{{smaller block|Ne ever rests he in ''tranquillity'',
The roring billowes beat his bowre so boystrously.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. x. 58.}}}}

{{smaller block|Preserving the ''tranquillity'' of our spirits and the evenness of our temper in the assault of infamy and disreputation. {{right|''Jer. Taylor'', Works (ed. 1835), I. 33.}}}}

{{smaller block|Power dwells apart in its ''tranquillity'',
Remote, serene, and inaccessible.
{{right|''Shelley'', Mont Blanc, iv.}}}}

=Syn. {{smaller|''Quiet, Peace'', etc. (see ''rest''<sup>1</sup>), serenity, placidness, calm, stillness.}}

