''scan.'' Cf. ''ascend'', ''descend''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1†.''' To climb over or up; ascend; mount; reach or extend upward to.

{{smaller block|The shore let her ''transcend'', the promont to descry.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, i. 71.}}}}

{{smaller block|It will be thought a thing ridiculous . . .
. . . that any poet, void
Of birth, or wealth, or temporal dignity,
Should with decorum ''transcend'' Cæsar's chair.
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Poetaster, v. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Make disquisition whether these unusual lights be meteorological impressions not ''transcending'' the upper region, or whether to be ranked among celestial bodies.
{{right|''Howell.'' (''Latham.'')}}}}

'''2.''' To pass over; go beyond; overpass; overstep.

{{smaller block|It is a dangerous opinion to such popes as shall ''transcend'' their limits and become tyrannical.
{{right|''Bacon.''}}}}

{{smaller block|The great will see that true love cannot be unrequited. True love ''transcends'' the unworthy object.
{{right|''Emerson'', Friendship, p. 206.}}}}

{{smaller block|We may indeed require rigid proof of whatever ''transcends'' our experience, but it is not only Orientals who say that "With God all things are possible."
{{right|''J. R. Seeley'', Nat. Religion, p. 77.}}}}

'''3.''' To surpass; outdo; excel; exceed.

{{smaller block|Secret scorching flames,
That far ''transcend'' earthly material fires,
Are crept into me, and there is no cure.
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', King and No King, iii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|High though her wit, yet humble was her mind;
As if she could not or she would not find
How much her worth ''transcended'' all her kind.
{{right|''Dryden'', Epitaph for Monument of a Lady at Bath.}}}}

'''4†.''' To cause to climb or pass; lift; elevate.
